WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.941
A marketplace moderates its listings with an agent.

00:00:05.241 --> 00:00:10.897
It gets a listing, four tools, check text, check image, check price, and publish the

00:00:10.897 --> 00:00:11.503
decision.

00:00:11.804 --> 00:00:15.548
And a prompt that says: review the listing and decide.

00:00:16.198 --> 00:00:18.670
Here are two traces from the same day.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:20.868
The first one looks perfect.

00:00:21.168 --> 00:00:23.658
Text, image, price, decision.

00:00:23.958 --> 00:00:26.256
Four turns, about eleven seconds.

00:00:26.906 --> 00:00:28.493
The second one is faster.

00:00:28.793 --> 00:00:33.845
Text, and then the model writes: the image looks consistent with the text.

00:00:34.145 --> 00:00:35.459
And it approves.

00:00:35.759 --> 00:00:37.437
The image check never ran.

00:00:38.087 --> 00:00:39.679
Look at that sentence again.

00:00:39.979 --> 00:00:43.812
The model gave an opinion on an image it never analyzed.

00:00:44.112 --> 00:00:47.484
And listing eighty-eight two fourteen had a banned image.

00:00:48.134 --> 00:00:51.958
Nobody on the team wanted the image check to be optional.

00:00:52.258 --> 00:00:56.183
The rule was clear: every listing goes through all three checks.

00:00:56.483 --> 00:00:58.785
But that rule lived in a prompt.

00:00:59.085 --> 00:01:03.173
And a prompt is a strong suggestion, not a guarantee.

00:01:03.473 --> 00:01:08.286
In this lesson, you'll learn to spot this smell in your traces, and the refactoring

00:01:08.286 --> 00:01:09.736
that removes it for good.

00:01:10.936 --> 00:01:12.293
Here's what's going on.

00:01:12.593 --> 00:01:16.746
Today, on every turn, the model picks which check to run next.

00:01:17.046 --> 00:01:18.156
Or whether to stop.

00:01:18.806 --> 00:01:22.308
So nothing guarantees that every check runs.

00:01:22.958 --> 00:01:25.055
But the task has a fixed path.

00:01:25.355 --> 00:01:28.461
In a chain, code always runs all three checks.

00:01:28.761 --> 00:01:34.072
And the model only steps in where judgment is actually needed: weighing the results

00:01:34.072 --> 00:01:34.904
and deciding.

00:01:35.554 --> 00:01:37.054
How do you recognize it?

00:01:37.354 --> 00:01:40.939
You can draw the task's flowchart, and it's always the same.

00:01:41.589 --> 00:01:44.457
The agent's prompt lists the steps in order.

00:01:44.757 --> 00:01:46.524
First do this, then that.

00:01:46.824 --> 00:01:50.606
If you're writing the order in the prompt, you already know the order.

00:01:51.256 --> 00:01:56.337
The traces show different orders, skipped steps, or repeated steps, for similar

00:01:56.337 --> 00:01:56.788
inputs.

00:01:57.438 --> 00:02:01.124
The number of turns varies a lot, with no business reason.

00:02:01.774 --> 00:02:06.890
And when it goes wrong, the explanation is: the agent thought it wasn't needed.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:09.893
Agents became the default.

00:02:10.193 --> 00:02:14.923
Frameworks and tutorials start with agents, and they look more modern than a

00:02:14.923 --> 00:02:16.292
sequence of functions.

00:02:16.942 --> 00:02:18.554
The demo convinces.

00:02:18.854 --> 00:02:22.448
In ten tests, the agent did everything in the right order.

00:02:22.748 --> 00:02:25.672
The variation shows up in the thousands of runs.

00:02:26.322 --> 00:02:28.339
Flexibility for the future.

00:02:28.639 --> 00:02:32.459
What if someday we want to skip the price check for some categories?

00:02:32.759 --> 00:02:38.159
Flexibility that might be useful someday costs reliability every day.

00:02:38.809 --> 00:02:41.053
And bundling everything looks simpler.

00:02:41.353 --> 00:02:46.800
One prompt and four tools look like less code than four chained functions.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.576
The code disappears.

00:02:48.876 --> 00:02:52.318
The complexity stays, hidden in the model's behavior.

00:02:53.518 --> 00:02:55.868
The cost starts with reliability.

00:02:56.168 --> 00:02:59.943
Steps that were supposed to be required quietly become optional.

00:03:00.243 --> 00:03:05.275
In moderation, compliance or finance, a skipped step is an incident.

00:03:05.925 --> 00:03:08.201
And small rates become real volume.

00:03:08.501 --> 00:03:12.597
Say the marketplace moderates fifty thousand listings a day.

00:03:12.897 --> 00:03:17.805
If the agent skips the image check on just zero point one percent of runs, that's

00:03:17.805 --> 00:03:21.682
fifty listings a day published with nobody looking at the photo.

00:03:22.332 --> 00:03:24.854
In a month, one thousand five hundred.

00:03:25.154 --> 00:03:29.425
A rate that looks great in the eval becomes real volume in production.

00:03:30.075 --> 00:03:30.932
Then money.

00:03:31.232 --> 00:03:36.546
Every agent turn resends the whole context, and pays for one more call to decide

00:03:36.546 --> 00:03:38.672
something the code already knew.

00:03:38.972 --> 00:03:44.329
In that perfect trace, three of the four model calls were only there to pick the

00:03:44.329 --> 00:03:44.999
next tool.

00:03:45.649 --> 00:03:46.595
Then latency.

00:03:46.895 --> 00:03:51.371
The checks run one after another, waiting for the model to choose.

00:03:51.671 --> 00:03:53.300
They could run at the same time.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.013
And testing.

00:03:55.313 --> 00:03:57.824
A fixed flow is easy to test.

00:03:58.124 --> 00:04:04.014
An agent needs a statistical eval just to prove it always does the same thing.

00:04:05.214 --> 00:04:06.637
Here's one for you.

00:04:06.937 --> 00:04:12.282
To guarantee the image check, the team wrote, in capital letters: always run check

00:04:12.282 --> 00:04:12.673
image.

00:04:12.973 --> 00:04:16.617
The skip rate fell from two percent to zero point three.

00:04:16.917 --> 00:04:18.274
Is the problem solved?

00:04:21.324 --> 00:04:22.067
No.

00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:24.649
The guarantee is still statistical.

00:04:24.949 --> 00:04:28.459
As long as the model decides, the step can be skipped.

00:04:28.759 --> 00:04:33.901
At fifty thousand listings a day, zero point three percent is one hundred fifty

00:04:33.901 --> 00:04:35.788
listings with no image check.

00:04:36.438 --> 00:04:39.160
And a better model won't get you to zero either.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:44.600
It lowers the rate again, but the problem is the design, not the model's ability.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:46.944
A required step has to be code.

00:04:47.244 --> 00:04:49.839
Then the rate is zero, by construction.

00:04:50.139 --> 00:04:55.472
Louder prompts also push you toward another smell, rule stacking, where all capital

00:04:55.472 --> 00:04:57.721
rules pile up and fight each other.

00:04:58.921 --> 00:05:03.271
The treatment is a refactoring called replace agent with chain.

00:05:03.571 --> 00:05:07.302
And the chain version of the moderator fits in a few lines.

00:05:07.952 --> 00:05:12.990
The three checks don't depend on each other, so code runs all three at the same

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:13.308
time.

00:05:13.958 --> 00:05:19.090
Then the model is called once, with the three results, to weigh them and decide.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:25.105
Now, skipping the image takes deleting a line of code, with review and tests.

00:05:25.405 --> 00:05:27.517
The model stays where it adds value.

00:05:27.817 --> 00:05:33.334
And latency becomes the slowest check, plus one model call, instead of four calls in

00:05:33.334 --> 00:05:33.728
a row.

00:05:34.378 --> 00:05:39.427
Then the team learns that vehicle listings need a fourth check, on the car's

00:05:39.427 --> 00:05:40.889
registration document.

00:05:41.189 --> 00:05:43.210
The other categories keep three.

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:46.663
Should you go back to the agent, since the path now varies?

00:05:47.313 --> 00:05:48.056
No.

00:05:48.356 --> 00:05:51.508
The path varies, but in a way you know before the run.

00:05:51.808 --> 00:05:56.933
That's a simple if on the category, or a separate route for vehicles.

00:05:57.233 --> 00:05:58.928
Known branches are routing.

00:05:59.228 --> 00:06:02.857
And each branch is still a chain, with every step guaranteed.

00:06:03.157 --> 00:06:08.334
Writing it into the prompt would just bring the same smell back, now on one branch.

00:06:09.534 --> 00:06:12.191
This smell isn't a rule against agents.

00:06:12.491 --> 00:06:17.278
When the path really depends on what you discover, an agent is the right tool.

00:06:17.578 --> 00:06:20.748
A support agent gets: the system is slow.

00:06:21.048 --> 00:06:27.293
Based on what it finds, it decides whether to look at logs, metrics, recent deploys

00:06:27.293 --> 00:06:28.497
or the database.

00:06:28.797 --> 00:06:29.984
That's not this smell.

00:06:30.284 --> 00:06:34.238
That's open investigation, the classic agent case.

00:06:34.538 --> 00:06:37.981
What it needs is a step budget and stop conditions.

00:06:38.631 --> 00:06:43.834
An agent can also be a good prototype, to discover which steps a task needs.

00:06:44.134 --> 00:06:47.845
When the traces start repeating, turn the path into code.

00:06:48.495 --> 00:06:53.880
And if skipping a step is truly acceptable, and cheap, the flexibility may be worth

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:54.074
it.

00:06:54.724 --> 00:06:57.006
One last case.

00:06:57.306 --> 00:07:02.030
A report agent always calls the same three tools, in the same order, and never

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:04.210
skipped one in six months of traces.

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:06.450
Is it still worth refactoring?

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:08.556
Yes, but with no rush.

00:07:08.856 --> 00:07:11.104
Its behavior is already a chain.

00:07:11.404 --> 00:07:17.493
Turning it into code cuts cost and latency, and trades a statistical guarantee for a

00:07:17.493 --> 00:07:18.146
real one.

00:07:18.446 --> 00:07:21.037
Do it with the next change to that flow.

00:07:22.237 --> 00:07:24.222
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:24.522 --> 00:07:28.469
If you can write the steps in the prompt, you can write them in code.

00:07:28.769 --> 00:07:32.368
And in code, a required step runs every single time.

00:07:32.668 --> 00:07:36.046
Keep the model for judgment, and keep the order for your code.

00:07:36.696 --> 00:07:41.540
In the next lesson, we close this trail with a real system that puts everything

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:44.360
together: a support bot with human escalation.

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:45.490
See you there.
